t \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134086 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\sqrt{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\sqrt{2}) & =⟨2,2\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\sqrt{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,2\\sqrt{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =((2\\sqrt{2})^{2}-{2}^{2})-({0}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(8-4)-(0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130660 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259148 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134089 1071 "![](images/36/36b985c811eb28d8220017c6b7407b3a104554d785dafdb35f17553c262bdc35.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}xydy$, where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-2)$ to the point $(2, 4)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨3t - 1, 6t - 2⟩$.*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134089 1071 "![](images/36/36b985c811eb28d8220017c6b7407b3a104554d785dafdb35f17553c262bdc35.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-2)$ to the point $(2,4),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩.$*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134089 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-2)$ and $(2,4).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-2+t(4-(-2)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,6t-2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)\\cdot (6t-2) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(18{t}^{2}-12t+2)\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-6t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130666 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259335 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134090 1071 "![](images/84/8417d2d62e9213ec3f92108495107fc982dc399e52cca8982e01516457f5692a.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}dy$, where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9x^{2} + 4y^{2} = 36$, traversed from the point $(2, 0)$ to the point $(0, 3)$, as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134090 1071 "![](images/84/8417d2d62e9213ec3f92108495107fc982dc399e52cca8982e01516457f5692a.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2y}{x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-right quarter of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134090 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130672 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259278 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}(x - y)dx$, where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16x^{2} + 25y^{2} = 400$, traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5, 0)$, as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨5\\cos t, 4\\sin t⟩$.*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134091 1071 "![](images/1b/1b693165c8f6201e96f0a972746324c65a308d1f143f229c465b6aa22b7aeb16.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+25{y}^{2}=400,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(5,0),$ as shown above?\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134091 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130684 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259194 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134093 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$ given that $F(x, y) = y^{2}i - x^{2}j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t) = ti + t^{2}j$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134093 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134093 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & ={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{2})^{2}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{4}\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & ={t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{4}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130690 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259176 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134094 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$ given that $F(x, y, z) = yzi + xzj + xyk$ and $C$ is the path $r(t) = ti + t^{2}j + t^{3}k$ for $t ∈ [0, 1]$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134094 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=yz\\,i+xz\\,j+xy\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134094 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y={t}^{2},\\,z={t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =yz\\,i+xz\\,j+xy\\,k \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,i+t\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,j+t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,k \\\\  & ={t}^{5}\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{5}\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & ={t}^{5}+2{t}^{5}+3{t}^{5} \\\\  & =6{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={{t}^{6}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130702 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186134096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259418 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186134096 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$ given that $F(x, y) = yi - xj$ and $C$ is the path $r(t) = \\cos ti + \\sin tj$ for 