3194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172294 1071 "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172294 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168609 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259136 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172296 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}ds$, where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r = \\sec θ$ for $\\frac{π}{6} \\le θ \\le \\frac{π}{3}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172296 1071 "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172296 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168627 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259371 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172299 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2} \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172299 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n1. $T_{1}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ v - u\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $T_{2}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u^{2} \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T_{3}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u + 2 \\\\ u - v + 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172299 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation ${T}_{1}$ is a linear transformation.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not affine since it contains the quadratic term ${u}^{2}.$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2 \\\\ u-v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168633 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259165 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172300 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=u-2v+1,\\,y=u+v-2\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172300 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = u - 2v + 1, y = u + v - 2$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172300 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}u-2v+1 \\\\ u+v-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-2v+1 \\\\ u+v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-2v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+(-2)+(-2)=-3.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168639 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259206 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172301 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=-u+av+b,\\,y=2u-5v+c\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172301 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x =-u + av + b, y = 2u - 5v + c$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172301 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+4v \\\\ 2u-5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+4v+2 \\\\ 2u-5v+1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-u+4v+2,\\,y=2u-5v+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+2+1=7.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168645 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259415 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172302 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v-7u+3 \\\\ 3v-u+1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv+1 \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}9u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172302 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n1. $T_{1}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v - 7u + 3 \\\\ 3v - u + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $T_{2}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}uv + 1 \\\\ u - 2v\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $T_{3}: \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}9u \\\\ u - v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172302 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-7u+3 \\\\ 3v-u+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+v \\\\ -u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is not affine since it contains the cross term $uv.$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation ${T}_{3}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{3}$ only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168651 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259373 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172303 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=au+4v,\\,y=-5u+bv+c\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\beg