 17592186045585 true] [17592186172304 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = 5u + 3, y = 7v - u$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 0 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172304 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5u+3 \\\\ 7v-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u+3 \\\\ 7v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u \\\\ -u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=5+(-1)+3=7.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168663 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259170 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172305 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = ⟨y,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0, 0)$ and $A(1, 1)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr = 0$, where $C_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $OA^(―)$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr = 0$, where $C_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y = x^{2}$, traversed from $O$ to $A$.\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$ is independent of path.\n4. $F$ is conservative." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172305 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n- $F$ is conservative." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172305 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168675 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259177 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172307 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = y^{2}e^{xy^{2}}i + 2xye^{xy^{2}}j$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\ln 2, 2)$ in $R^{2}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172307 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+2xy{e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172307 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y{e}^{x{y}^{2}}(1+x{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{2}}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{2}^{4}}-1 \\\\  & ={2}^{4}-1 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186168687 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259053 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172309 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) =-2xy\\sin x^{2}i + \\cos x^{2}j$. Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{π}, 1)$ in $R^{2}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186172309 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186172309 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(0,0)$ and $B(\\sqrt{\\pi },1).$\n\nNotice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative. Therefore, we know there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\nReconstructing $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{\\pi },1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2}-(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186176319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186174142 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186176319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259392 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186176319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186176319 1071 "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x, y) = ⟨x^{2}\\cos x + y^{3}, ye^{y + 1} + x⟩$, which of the following is equivalent to $∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186176319 1071 "![](images/8b/8ba69d7bfe8dd363288bbf86661172004b21143f02c70260da0c184f066a7c12.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3},\\,y{e}^{y+1}+x⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186176319 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx+(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b90abecc774b5ba5dcee19385fef38b14206d2c31c3b6cba38216bfa9957448.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=y{e}^{y+1}+x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{3})\\,dx-(y{e}^{y+1}+x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(1-3\\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-3{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259523 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259524 1319413