6179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259655 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259657 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259664 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259665 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259666 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179778 1038 #edb/ref 17592186259667 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177579 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259475 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179990 1071 "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(6x^{2} - y^{2})ds$, where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6x^{2} + y^{2} = 6$, shown above?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179990 1071 "![](images/0b/0b0f3ae9a464812957c297c09ff3f65bfe5964bdac70298e72ec3dbde2325e72.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the line integral ${\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $6{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=6,$ shown above?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179990 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $\\sqrt{6},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =6{cos}^{2}⁡t-6{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(6{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177591 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259406 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179992 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = \\ln (x + y)$, traversed along the line segment from $(1, 3)$ to $(0, 3)$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179992 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179992 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177609 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259199 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179995 1071 "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u+v-2,\\,y=u-2v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(3,2)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179995 1071 "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n$x = 3u + v - 2, y = u - 2v + 1$.\nLet $P(3, 2)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point $P^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of $P^{′}$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179995 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v-2 \\\\ u-2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+v \\\\ u-2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(9,0).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177633 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259255 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179999 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=0$\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186179999 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$x = u - v, y = u + 2v + 1$.\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $det(A) = 0$\n3. The transformation is invertible" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186179999 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177639 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259295 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180000 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-y+1,\\,v=by-x+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180000 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$x = u + v, y = u + 2v + 1$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n$u = ax - y + 1, v = by - x + c$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180000 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot x+1\\cdot (y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-y+1 \\\\ y-x-1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-y+1,\\,v=1y-x+(-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+1+(-1)=2.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177645 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259243 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180001 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180001 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind $a + b + c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180001 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+1+(-8)=-9.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177663 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259321 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180004 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k + 2)$! for any $k \\ge 0$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180004 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $(k+2)!$ for any $k\\ge 0?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180004 1074 "Using the definition of factorial, we have \n\n$$\n(k+2)!=(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nNow, applying the factorial definition to all but the first factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+2)! & =(k+2)\\cdot \\overset{(k+1)!}{\\overset{⏞}{(k+1)\\cdot k\\cdot \\,⋯\\,\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}} \\\\  & =(k+2)\\cdot (k+1)!\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177669 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259236 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180005 1071 "![](images/ac/ac639fb08d54d2004aec96708ffd19bc080b028e739075687a84a09158cd4fd2.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}dx$, where $C$ is the path along a semicircle of radius $2$, traversed from the point $(2, 1)$ to the point $(-2, 1)$, shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180005 1071 "![](images/ac/ac639fb08d54d2004aec96708ffd19bc080b028e739075687a84a09158cd4fd2.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a semicircle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,1)$ to the point $(-2,1),$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180005 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=-2$ and ${x}_{1}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=(-2)-2=-4.