t $(0,3),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180007 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{2}=3$ and ${y}_{1}=0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=3-0=3.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177687 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259185 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180008 1071 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)dx = 4$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx = 6$, calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}f(x, y)dx$ given that $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180008 1071 "If ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=4$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=6,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180008 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177705 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259407 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180011 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind $a+b+c,$ given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180011 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation $T$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFind $a + b + c$, given that the matrix form of the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 5 \\\\ b & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180011 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix form of $T$ gives us the following information:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 7-(-5)\\cdot (4)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=3+4+3=10.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177711 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259272 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180012 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5v-7u+3,\\,y=7v-10u+5.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-5y+4,\\,v=bx+cy+5,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180012 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$x = 5v - 7u + 3, y = 7v - 10u + 5$.\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation $T^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n$u = ax - 5y + 4, v = bx + cy + 5$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180012 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5v-7u+3 \\\\ 7v-10u+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u+5v \\\\ -10u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -10 & 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot (-7)-5\\cdot (-10)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-3 \\\\ y-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot (x-3)+(-5)\\cdot (y-5) \\\\ 10\\cdot (x-3)+(-7)\\cdot (y-5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7x-5y+4 \\\\ 10x-7y+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7x-5y+4,\\,v=10x+(-7)y+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+10+(-7)=10.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177723 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259172 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180014 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy$, where $C$ is part of the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1, 0)$ to the point $B(0, 1)$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180014 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180014 1074 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177729 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259402 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180015 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $MN^(―)$ traversed from the point $M(-1, 0)$ to the point $N(2, 4)$, then $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)ydx - xdy =$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180015 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then ${\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180015 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-(-i)) \\\\  & =-i+t(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\,i+4t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t-1$ and $y(t)=4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3)-(3t-1)(4)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,dt \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177741 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259483 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180017 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = (x + y)i + π\\cos πyj$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3, 1)$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180017 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(-3,1).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180017 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i-j$ and $b=-3\\,i+j.$\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =(i-j)+t[-3\\,i+j-(i-j)] \\\\  & =(i-j)+t(-4\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =(1-4t)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t=1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=1-4t,\\,y=2t-1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y\\,j \\\\  & =(1-4t+(2t-1))\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-2t\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,j)\\cdot (-4\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =-2t\\cdot (-4)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8t+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )\\,dt \\\\  & ={4{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2t-1)\\pi )|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =(4+0)-(0+0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177759 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259119 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180020 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)2ydx + \\ln xdy + 3zdz$, where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t) = i + tj + t^{2}k$ for $t ∈ [1, 3]$?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180020 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180020 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1,\\,y(t)=t,\\,z(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+3z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2y\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2t\\cdot (0)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\cdot (1)+3{t}^{2}\\cdot (2t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}6{t}^{2}\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177771 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259096 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180022 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot dr$, where $F(x, y) = \\cos xi + yj$ and $C$ is the curve $y = 2\\sin^{2} x$ traversed from the point $A(0, 0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{π}{2}, 2)$." 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180022 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180022 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Since $y$ is the subject of the equation, a parametrization is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2{sin}^{2}⁡t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{2}⁡t)j \\\\  & =i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\text{ })\\cdot (i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{sin}^{4}⁡t]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177789 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259111 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180025 1071 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr = 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr = 0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180025 1071 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180025 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177795 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259356 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180026 1071 "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x, y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 false] [17592186180026 1071 "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186180026 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the par