071 "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=t,\\,0\\le t\\le 4\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259571 1072 #uuid "6698ec8a-fca5-5069-8adb-1cdf8da61054" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259571 1074 "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=2\\sqrt{t}.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2.83$ | $3.46$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/89/898075d0f74bdfcbab28e50ee327770400f6f58d82728cd8bf55cc75e852d161.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259229 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1069 "q-12380" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1071 "The third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=0$ is" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1072 #uuid "a9ad2113-d832-55e5-9986-19475965d691" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259572 1074 "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!} \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x+{f}^{″}(0)\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{f}^{‴}(0)\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & f(0) & ={e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & ⟹ &  & {f}^{‴}(0) & =-2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ ${f}^{″}(0),$ and ${f}^{‴}(0)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x) & =1+(-1)\\cdot x+0\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+(2)\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{6} \\\\  & =1-x+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259332 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1069 "q-12383" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1071 "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{\\pi x/2}$ about $x=0$ is" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1072 #uuid "e7110f0c-3dfd-53a8-84e2-2145947a7cf3" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259573 1074 "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{e}^{\\pi x/2} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot x+(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}x+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259257 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1069 "q-123839" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1071 "Let $R=[-1,3]\\times [2,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=-3$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1072 #uuid "089ded76-3ed3-5ada-805a-37477a513dd6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259574 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=-3,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-3)\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =-3({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =-3(3-(-1))(5-2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=-36\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le -36=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-36.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259489 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1069 "q-123843" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1071 "Let $R=[-5,-2]\\times [3,7]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=1$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1072 #uuid "bde095b1-d9b2-5272-b46e-003fde137023" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259575 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}1\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =(-2-(-5))(7-3) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=12\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 12=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=12.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259384 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1069 "q-123846" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1071 "Let $R=[1,7]\\times [4,6]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}},\\,{P}_{2}={{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is a function such that ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=6$ for all partitions $P$ of $R,$ and ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ respectively in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1072 #uuid "198f001c-8f0a-5ac7-9854-7515cac5441b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259576 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P),\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R,$ where the lower and upper sums respectively are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{M}_{ij}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand ${m}_{ij}$ and ${M}_{ij}$ are the minimum and maximum values of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ \n\nNow, since ${m}_{ij}={M}_{ij}=6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}6\\,Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =6({\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}) \\\\  & =6(7-1)(6-4) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=72\\le {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\le 72=U(f,P)\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=72.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259147 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1069 "q-123872" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1071 "Let $R=[-4,0]\\times [-5,-2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-2{x}_{i}-5{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-2{x}_{i-1}-5{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1072 #uuid "577234f6-db60-5e1b-860e-d2e2b0e4c7da" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259577 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{i}-5{y}_{j}\\le -2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -2{x}_{i-1}-5{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{i}-5{y}_{j}\\le -({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{5}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -2{x}_{i-1}-5{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{5}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le -{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}-\\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({0}^{2}-(-4)^{2})(-2-(-5))=-48 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{5}{2}((-2)^{2}-(-5)^{2})(0-(-4))=-210\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le -(-48)-(-210)\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 258\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=258.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259082 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1069 "q-123873" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1071 "Let $R=[-1,2]\\times [-2,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-3-4{x}_{i}-6{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(-3-4{x}_{i-1}-6{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1072 #uuid "10e4111c-3c40-57e4-9d64-266a3dc148ce" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259578 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-4{x}_{i}-6{y}_{j} & \\le -3-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j}) \\\\  & \\le -3-4{x}_{i-1}-6{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n-3-4{x}_{i}-6{y}_{j}\\le -3-2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-3({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -3-4{x}_{i-1}-6{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[-3-2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-3({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le -3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2})(2-(-2))=24 \\\\ 3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =3({2}^{2}-(-2)^{2})(2-(-1))=0 \\\\ 3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =3(2-(-1))(2-(-2))=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le -36-24-0\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le -60\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-60.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259279 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1069 "q-123882" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1071 "Which of the following regions is *not* connected?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1072 #uuid "856afbeb-9f59-56e5-affb-452bd8050ce2" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259579 1074 "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/10/1067a6abc2249b31f3786a3f277a2db10d8f32cbacf971a550a0473243684da7.png)\n\n \n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcaaf15d709d6396155bcdc9e8976bb037c9f341fc0b8863687958dbb0f40ff.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259178 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1069 "q-123885" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1071 "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1072 #uuid "de4d0c85-6915-5695-9beb-a379a8504775" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259580 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following: \n\n![](images/4a/4a67c845fe7993fe0c91784c92201db6ff5c15098db04dc588f5ebe0e8cdc1a1.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259301 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1069 "q-123887" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n- ${R}^{2}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<4}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1072 #uuid "43a2cfcc-f62a-5511-8a9e-2111b0d76d4f" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259581 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region (a circle) with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259289 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1069 "q-123892" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1071 "Which of the following gives the area bounded by graph of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1072 #uuid "ad821977-71ab-5f6d-b217-d1c16f07961b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259582 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{2\\pi /3}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259296 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1069 "q-123894" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1071 "![](images/7a/7a48288304608aa9d2b68a34794183cf58308ce16487c1675318180936868674.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1072 #uuid "8353f56b-3cb6-596c-be65-c81f93c84a5f" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259583 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\pi $ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{5\\pi /4}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{5\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{5\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\theta {|}_{\\pi }^{5\\pi /4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ){|}_{\\pi }^{5\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}((\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-(\\pi ))+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\pi }{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259393 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1069 "q-123916" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (2,1)}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>25}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1072 #uuid "beac61f6-0d9e-58bf-85d2-405f0693eb4d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259584 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn. \n\n- Region I is simply connected. It's a plane region (a circle) without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(2,1).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 25.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259258 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1069 "q-123923" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,(x,y)\\ne (0,0)}$\n- ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ge 0}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1072 #uuid "cd4cc5a1-c047-51e7-8736-2282f38e56c0" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259585 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is *not* simply connected. The region contains all the points of ${R}^{2}$ except those on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Thus, the region is formed by two disjoint pieces and, therefore, is not even a connected region.\n\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region III is simply connected. It's a plane region without holes.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259162 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1069 "q-123984" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j$\n- $G=3{e}^{3x+2y}\\,i+2{e}^{3x+2y}\\,j$\n- $H=2xy\\,i+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1072 #uuid "dc1786af-49ed-5f49-98af-90852a6f6e8b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259586 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=3{e}^{3x+2y}$ and $Q=2{e}^{3x+2y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6{e}^{3x+2y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6{e}^{3x+2y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2xy$ and $Q={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259431 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1069 "q-123993" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{3}-ay)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-2x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1072 #uuid "b7768381-49c0-5755-a526-0e09cbb087b9" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259587 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{3}-ay,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-a & =3{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ -a & =-2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259105 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1069 "q-123998" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{2}+2xy)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2axy)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1072 #uuid "ca502a8a-0b55-5b4d-8a4b-58f7420e37da" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259588 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{2}+2xy,\\,Q={x}^{2}+2axy.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y+2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+2ay.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+2x & =2x+2ay \\\\ 2y & =2ay \\\\ 2y-2ay & =0 \\\\ 2y(1-a) & =0 \\\\ 1-a & =0 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259430 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1069 "q-124000" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1071 "Consider the vector field $F=(ax{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1072 #uuid "652885e3-f092-5423-886c-a33306bd20ca" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259589 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=ax{y}^{2}+1,\\,Q=4{x}^{2}y+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2axy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8xy.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2axy & =8xy \\\\ 2axy-8xy & =0 \\\\ 2(a-4)xy & =0 \\\\ a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equality must be satisfied everywhere on $D,$ we conclude that $a-4=0,$ that is, $a=4.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259060 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1069 "q-124002" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1072 #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259590 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259073 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1069 "q-124003" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{e}^{2x}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,3{t}^{2}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1072 #uuid "c23e8787-68ef-58ab-bf57-8f769ed64353" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259591 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =(3{e}^{2\\cdot 1})-(0\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-0 \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259086 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1069 "q-124005" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,2t⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1072 #uuid "615b0135-e131-5b5f-9638-4296b1aa2a4a" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259592 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩ \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$ and $(\\pi ,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,2\\pi )-f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ) \\\\  & =(2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\pi )-(\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259210 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1069 "q-124008" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1072 #uuid "26e83fe0-4239-5444-b048-589ba5747a08" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259593 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259108 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1069 "q-124027" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y+1}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={e}^{x}+1$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1072 #uuid "530f83ac-873a-5207-ba3d-e9438dabfd29" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259594 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{t}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,2)$ and $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,3)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {e}^{3+1}-0\\cdot {e}^{2+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259135 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1069 "q-124028" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1072 #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259595 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259239 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1069 "q-124029" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1072 #uuid "2e457dc8-8930-562f-905f-f48a2ebaba17" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259596 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259144 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1069 "q-124030" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\in [1,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1072 #uuid "33d927c3-e1a0-5a24-bd3b-65369e4fc02e" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259597 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,4].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩, \\\\ r(4) & =⟨4,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(4,4)-f(2,1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}+{e}^{4\\cdot 4})-({2}^{2}+{e}^{2\\cdot 1}) \\\\  & =(16+{e}^{16})-(4+{e}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{16}-{e}^{2}+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259366 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1069 "q-124032" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1072 #uuid "1b4571c4-6fd7-5557-9855-d69404faa890" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259598 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259423 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1069 "q-124034" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1071 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1072 #uuid "cc34530a-2e34-53d7-8fcc-b9ff1814815f" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259599 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259081 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1069 "q-124035" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1071 "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1072 #uuid "5c2ccfca-31f7-5fd8-9901-48e158e0be78" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259600 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259346 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1069 "q-124047" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1071 "The curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ intersects the line $y=2x$ at the points $(p,q)$ and $(r,s).$ What is $p+q+r+s?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1072 #uuid "cfd68690-a442-5c36-9d86-3537048ffd62" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259601 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x}-1 \\\\ y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}-1 & =2x \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{x} & =2x \\\\ 1-x & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x=-1$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ To find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the second equation.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$ \n\nFinally, if we let $p=-1,q=-2,r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $s=1,$ then\n\n$$\np+q+r+s=-1-2+\\frac{1}{2}+1=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a2420b1e390edd61efd28a223cd1beb93db98609a8d31c69526434e7188658c.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259184 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1069 "q-12406" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{(n!)^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1072 #uuid "ca290c9b-3571-5402-b278-a1e324876197" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259602 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{2}{x}^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{2(n+1)+1}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n!)^{2}}{{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n!)^{2}}{((n+1)!)^{2}}\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =|{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259471 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1069 "q-12409" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1071 "Find the interval of convergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{(\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\\,.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1072 #uuid "603add58-8ab9-54cf-90bc-6ce9518f13ee" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259603 1074 "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}| \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{n}}{(n+1)^{n+1}} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1}{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-n} \\\\  & =|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{e(n+1)} \\\\  & =|x|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259417 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1069 "q-124144" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1071 "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=-v+u+2,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(4,1)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1072 #uuid "b1f2af36-2cc7-54c9-8c5f-1fbb3c54f92a" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259604 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-v+u+2 \\\\ u+v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(5,4).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259470 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1069 "q-124151" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1071 "![](images/33/33f7326ffe8318fca915e8cc7e02a2a4c280dd9377066f1870f3bb8067b22eb0.png)\n\nConsider the segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, and the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u+3v+2,\\,y=v-2u.\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ under the action of $T$ is the segment $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}$ in the $xy$-plane. Which of the following diagrams shows $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1072 #uuid "02e13286-8600-5387-b2b8-d8f440c7b1cc" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259605 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+3v+2 \\\\ v-2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,2u+3v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-5,3)$ and $(10,0).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/e4/e4bea3edd3fcde575e68e36e08fc4186863b12450802eb0ee37c841975983486.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259044 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1069 "q-124156" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1071 "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3v+u,\\,y=u-6v+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(-2,3)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1072 #uuid "e5dd167d-b815-5ffc-9ae4-cf52473638b6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259606 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3v+u \\\\ u-6v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u-6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(7,-17).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259316 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1069 "q-124166" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1071 "The line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$ for $k\\ne 0,$ only once. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1072 #uuid "cbecf5f3-3192-5842-9f4d-79b30b51a4fe" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259607 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=2x$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x-2},$  we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ 2x(x-2) & =k \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x-k & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-4)\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4(2)(-k)}}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{16+8k}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola only once, the quadratic formula above must give only one real solution.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16+8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =-16 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $k=-2.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259359 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1069 "q-124167" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1071 "![](images/55/559a6b6cb717c10e60b77da402e310fc5895c814655b4ca1748eba294d712a32.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{2}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper half of the circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1072 #uuid "0853e6c9-d856-5aec-b1f2-e5d65484f5a2" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259608 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a half of a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[(1)^{3}-(-1)^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259140 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1069 "q-124169" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1071 "The line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ where $k\\ne 0,$ twice. What is the range of possible values of  $k?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1072 #uuid "a15074ac-c1b5-52b9-98eb-18ca5c6abeb6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259609 1074 "To determine whether the line $y=x-k$ intersects the hyperbola $y=\\frac{k}{x}-k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x-k &  \\\\ y=\\frac{k}{x}-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and simplifying, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-k & =\\frac{k}{x}-k \\\\ x-k & =\\frac{k-kx}{x} \\\\ x(x-k) & =k-kx \\\\ {x}^{2} & =k \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the line to intersect the hyperbola at two points, the quadratic formula above must give two real solutions.\n\nSo, the expression inside the square root must be positive: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k\\in (0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259071 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1069 "q-124173" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1071 "![](images/db/dbd3fdd67ac209544717fc7d4b33831c7cdfd9f6839a7c1410f43ffd385d63ff.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(0,-1),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1072 #uuid "105c34ef-d15b-56de-a1a8-f1b42bfb4fee" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259610 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}}{8y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{16({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8t\\,{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259161 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1069 "q-124175" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1071 "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(\\sqrt{x-1}+y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1072 #uuid "bd888ac9-9398-5cd7-abd9-7da37aaa7d68" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259611 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x-1}+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t}-t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259448 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1069 "q-124177" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1071 "![](images/27/272b0afabca579f65cbaa7949fd1be70427ff0fd860eb953207bbc8eda7b6e9c.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{3x}{y}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the upper-left quarter of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1072 #uuid "0f008788-4322-589f-b4f5-5bd6b468b9b0" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259612 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259070 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1069 "q-124178" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1071 "![](images/af/afc9e81f3294803794ba0d62773958913cf7475a0718e77052212a149050aecf.png)\n\nConsider the segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ above, and the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u+2v-2,\\,y=v-u+2.\n$$\n\nThe image of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ under the action of $T$ is the segment $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}$ in the $xy$-plane. Which of the following diagrams shows $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1072 #uuid "7e89e4f1-f018-5737-8c5b-79e211b0f9fe" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259613 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+2v-2 \\\\ v-u+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+2v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-6,2)$ and $(8,1).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/8e/8e57b4d47ef3c6ad1b1d70a97126aa865c4481ee67e16eaf4e79c09e3146b30d.png)" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259396 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1069 "q-124179" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=3$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=5,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1072 #uuid "42bfa60d-7b26-5dc6-9634-a2e58886024d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259614 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3+5 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259115 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1069 "q-124180" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-1$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=4,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1072 #uuid "f705291d-0bfe-5456-9170-c496d39786bb" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259615 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(-1)-4 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259167 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1069 "q-124181" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=8$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1072 #uuid "41e596da-5347-5fcf-affc-7dc004feff07" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259616 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-8-(-3) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259282 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1069 "q-124182" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=14$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=10,$ calculate ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1072 #uuid "9aaf57c2-f732-5f6e-81fd-8a42d073c397" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259617 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-14+10 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259219 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1069 "q-124184" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1071 "If ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=15$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=10,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1072 #uuid "caa68865-4fa8-5716-bcf1-19dd497b3997" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259618 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 15 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 15 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-10 \\\\ 25 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-25.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259312 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1069 "q-124185" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=9$ and ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-3,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1072 #uuid "9a9a0ed6-350a-5e53-8c64-58cd4ca588af" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259619 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ -{\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & =-3-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ -9 & =-3-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ -6 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=6.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259439 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1069 "q-124186" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=16$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=11,$ calculate ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1072 #uuid "7673d1a0-afd2-5d22-93cd-75c43bd54a25" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259620 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -16 & =-11+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -5 & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-5.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259085 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1069 "q-124188" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1071 "If ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=21,$ ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=2,$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-11,$ calculate ${\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1072 #uuid "8ad1a2a8-0a25-5345-b64f-001fe66a3826" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259621 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -{\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & =2-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -21 & =2-(-11)+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -21 & =13+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -34 & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x=-34.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259134 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1069 "q-124192" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v+u+1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-3$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1072 #uuid "d0a1ba57-9eac-5d49-9cdd-f7f25fec8c92" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259622 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v+u+1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+2v \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259422 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1069 "q-124201" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1071 "![](images/34/34f6562d5a661d3a58755622571f50752aefa11c8e58b85dad5c70b13c179746.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta +1}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1072 #uuid "a5dfdc65-7bed-5a91-b76e-2d2d570b57d3" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259623 1074 "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta +1}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta +1})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta +2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2\\pi +2}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2}({e}^{2\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259408 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1069 "q-124210" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1071 "Which of the following affine transformations are invertible?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1072 #uuid "72479cb8-eb92-5b20-8e4e-e8b5aa32f2b9" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259624 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThe transformation is invertible if the matrix $A$ is invertible. That is, if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v-5 \\\\ 10u-4v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-2v \\\\ 10u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -4\\end{aligned}|=-4\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot 10=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{1}$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is invertible. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v-4 \\\\ u+5v+7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 1=6\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is nonzero, ${T}_{2}$ is invertible.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not invertible. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v-2 \\\\ 3u+6v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u+4v \\\\ 3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}|=6\\cdot 2-4\\cdot 3=0.\n$$\n\nSince the determinant is zero, ${T}_{3}$ is *not* invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{2}$ only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259436 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1069 "q-124212" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1071 "![](images/df/dfa95100566fce9ab113b00ae53d1a76e8977b68aa4bac0e9b6892a9930b70d4.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the lower half of the circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1072 #uuid "cdf631e5-2841-5a67-a8ff-d0eebc8b7e83" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259625 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{3}}{8(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\operatorname{sin}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}{32\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-8[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ] \\\\  & =-8(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259394 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1069 "q-124213" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-6v-2,\\,y=5v-u+9.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=4$\n- The transformation is invertible" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1072 #uuid "3230d0ef-3334-5e9c-bc28-426c4388c6b0" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259626 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v-2 \\\\ 5v-u+9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-6v \\\\ -u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\ne [\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|=5\\cdot 2-(-6)\\cdot (-1)=4\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \