gin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259646 1072 #uuid "ded880b4-9f04-5275-85c1-4486642a0a7d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259646 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+8v \\\\ u\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6u+8v-2 \\\\ u+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=6u+8v-2,\\,y=u+5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=6+8+5=19.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259063 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1069 "q-124262" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=au+v,\\,y=bu+cv\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1072 #uuid "18f13e13-3246-593c-9da3-b608f58de1b5" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259647 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u+v \\\\ 9u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2u+v \\\\ 9u+3v\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-2u+v,\\,y=9u+3v\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-2+9+3=10.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259182 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1069 "q-124263" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=4u+av-2,\\,y=bu+cv-3\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1072 #uuid "569ad093-da2b-5144-992e-0f84b411665b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259648 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-v \\\\ 9u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-v-2 \\\\ 9u+5v-3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=4u+(-1)v-2,\\,y=9u+5v-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-1+9+5=13.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259447 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1069 "q-124265" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=au+6v+b,\\,y=cu-4v\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1072 #uuid "e491209f-8e14-5a51-846e-3087f73ab2f3" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259649 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+6v \\\\ 3u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+6v \\\\ 3u-4v\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=1u+6v+0,\\,y=3u-4v\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+0+3=4.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259203 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1069 "q-124267" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=au-2v+b,\\,y=4u+cv-2\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1072 #uuid "9cc9e110-657b-5c50-af6f-2ba6688d538a" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259650 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6u-2v \\\\ 4u+7v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6u-2v+1 \\\\ 4u+7v-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-6u-2v+1,\\,y=4u+7v-2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-6+1+7=2.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259334 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1069 "q-124270" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1071 "![](images/58/58995f1bc5b45dbe0d30a9d0cbf704d792b955e7195c0e4dc3610ddf2e576579.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-1,-1)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩.$*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1072 #uuid "0410ccfc-9f5a-5741-9bf7-f1f601669aaa" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259651 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-1+t(2-(-1))),\\,(-1+t(1-(-1)))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3t-1,\\,2t-1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t-1)^{2}-(2t-1) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-6t+1-2t+1 \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}-8t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}-8t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259311 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1069 "q-124273" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1071 "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1072 #uuid "11aa222d-e9ea-5ecd-ab97-032f5281bcab" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259652 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){e}^{1-{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={cos}^{2}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{e}^{{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259428 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1069 "q-124276" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+7v+2,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-7y+3,\\,v=by+cx-2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1072 #uuid "4865ae47-4fd4-593e-a597-ec2a2d5aa88e" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259653 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+7v+2 \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u+7v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4-7\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+(-7)\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (x-2)+4\\cdot (y-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-7y+3 \\\\ 4y-x-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-7y+3,\\,v=4y+(-1)x-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+4+(-1)=5.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259281 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1069 "q-124279" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1071 "![](images/8d/8d55ece1948f9ecb7da7d489b86a297651a34f409f83ba2470f6dd06678a375e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(0,-3)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1072 #uuid "7eef0fe8-9d36-5fca-a5de-d79c02a74295" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259654 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259479 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1069 "q-124280" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2-u-v,\\,y=3u+4v-1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=7+ax-y,\\,v=bx+y+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1072 #uuid "f20eb077-8cae-5138-ae0e-248f60ff9fb8" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259655 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u-v \\\\ 3u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-2)+(-1)\\cdot (y+1) \\\\ 3\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7-4x-y \\\\ 3x+y-5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=7+(-4)x-y,\\,v=3x+y+(-5).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+3+(-5)=-6.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259322 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1069 "q-124281" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1071 "![](images/6b/6b095a29b433107c37cd7e28c853d9d3d3547fb64a9e874783206b86c0aca6c1.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(1,-3)$ to the point $(4,0),$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1072 #uuid "5026f273-e6ca-5d94-86d8-dd1bd2a406be" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259656 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $x$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $x$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${x}_{1}$ is the $x$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${x}_{2}=4$ and ${x}_{1}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x={x}_{2}-{x}_{1}=4-1=3.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259327 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1069 "q-124282" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-v+1,\\,y=9u-4v+3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+a,\\,v=by+cx+3,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1072 #uuid "ab7706f1-5139-5e95-9482-c097bef51185" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259657 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v+1 \\\\ 9u-4v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-v \\\\ 9u-4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-3) \\\\ -9\\cdot (x-1)+2\\cdot (y-3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y-4x+1 \\\\ 2y-9x+3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=y-4x+1,\\,v=2y+(-9)x+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+2+(-9)=-6.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259391 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1069 "q-124284" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx=4v-u,\\,y=3v-7\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}c \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1072 #uuid "31239ffb-2521-5f24-adb5-1bf7dc8cd57c" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259658 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4v-u \\\\ 3v-7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSeparating the 