17592186259665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259665 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=2u-3v+2,\\,y=6v-3u-4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=x+by+c,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259665 1072 #uuid "6d452c80-d748-5af8-bbed-4f41e3a6ab8b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259665 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v+2 \\\\ 6v-3u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2u-3v \\\\ -3u+6v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-2 \\\\ y+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x-2)+1\\cdot (y+4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x-2)+(\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (y+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y \\\\ x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+0,\\,v=x+\\frac{2}{3}y+\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259385 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1069 "q-124315" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=5u-9v-2,\\,y=4u-8v+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=ax-\\frac{9}{4}y+b,\\,v=cx-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1072 #uuid "2c660cc6-fa1c-504a-b604-d474fb35da3e" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259666 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v-2 \\\\ 4u-8v+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u-9v \\\\ 4u-8v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & -9 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-8\\cdot 5-(-9)\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-4}[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 1 & -\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+2 \\\\ y-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+2)-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot (y-4) \\\\ 1\\cdot (x+2)+(-\\frac{5}{4})\\cdot (y-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13 \\\\ x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x-\\frac{9}{4}y+13,\\,v=1x-\\frac{5}{4}y+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+13+1=16.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259054 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1069 "q-124316" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1071 "The affine transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=-2v-4,\\,y=u+4v+6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ of $T$ is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+a,\\,v=bx+cy-2,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1072 #uuid "599d880d-d7f3-5208-a64f-d6b9b8d46ef7" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259667 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nReversing the action of $T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au+b & =x \\\\ Au & =x-b \\\\ u & ={A}^{-1}(x-b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing our transformation in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v-4 \\\\ u+4v+6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2v \\\\ u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the inverse of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u} & ={{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}}\\cdot ({{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}-{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x+4 \\\\ y-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot (x+4)+1\\cdot (y-6) \\\\ (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (x+4)+0\\cdot (y-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, the inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}$ in coordinate form is\n\n$$\nu=2x+y+2,\\,v=(-\\frac{1}{2})x+0y-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2+(-\\frac{1}{2})+0=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259403 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1069 "q-124321" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=2v-u+2,\\,y=2u+v-5.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=-5$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1072 #uuid "d6b75fa0-1178-5417-bc31-861bbdef5229" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259668 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2v-u+2 \\\\ 2u+v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2=-5.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259222 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1069 "q-124322" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+3v-5,\\,y=2v+u-4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=1$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1072 #uuid "8bc55bfe-e692-5546-9ff3-60dd10f588d6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259669 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v-5 \\\\ 2v+u-4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+3v \\\\ u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259458 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1069 "q-124324" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u-3v-5,\\,y=-2u+2v+4.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $det(A)=2$\n- The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1072 #uuid "0642bf2f-73a7-542a-b570-be0c81694e35" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259670 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-3v-5 \\\\ -2u+2v+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4u-3v \\\\ -2u+2v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|=4\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-2)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259319 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259671 1069 "q-12