}$ is the path comprising of a quarter circle of radius $2$ and a line segment, as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259679 1072 #uuid "a93c4294-5e42-5221-bf50-6a3ab53be754" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259679 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{1}=2$ and ${y}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=(-1)-2=-3.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259434 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1069 "q-124341" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1071 "![](images/1c/1c074157bb6374a4da01d6c1312bb60520966317b1f903eb44a191384e398ae6.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of a quarter circle of radius $3$ and a line segment, as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1072 #uuid "c941258b-2cc1-58e8-aec4-791b92e6f425" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259680 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{1}=1$ and ${y}_{2}=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=(-4)-1=-5.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259209 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1069 "q-124342" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1071 "![](images/9d/9df3b88fb441956592ef8c45214c4728bb321f6fd6b627eb67ce3ce963438915.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of a vertical line segment of length $5$ and a semicircle of radius $4,$ as  shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1072 #uuid "62aa34a7-e962-5ffb-a115-12676e1e5f66" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259681 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{1}=4$ and  ${y}_{2}=-1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=(-1)-4=-5.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259252 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1069 "q-124345" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the exact solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ and ${x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{4}?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1072 #uuid "af7fb4a9-e4d6-59e9-abfc-0dd8490eaf10" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259682 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(1+6n)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ and ${x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{19\\pi }{12}=\\frac{20\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259075 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1069 "q-124346" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1071 "Calculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the closed path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1072 #uuid "50090625-8265-554a-a80a-431c6d790bbe" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259683 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, the initial and terminal points are the same (since the path is closed and is traversed only once). Therefore, ${y}_{1}={y}_{2},$ and we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259464 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1069 "q-124347" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1071 "![](images/03/0361756c09e5f72ddb040240bbec492c0a9dd3562307253ae31bece95bb41d88.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$  where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ is the path comprising of two line segments and a semicircle or radius $4,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1072 #uuid "9c15b831-9542-5e2b-8769-835aae0b933e" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259684 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, the initial and terminal points are the same (since the path is closed and is traversed only once). Therefore, ${y}_{1}={y}_{2},$ and we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y=0.\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259083 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259685 1069 "q-124348" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259685 1071 "![](images/9b/9b3065e58decfca5355252b93ec3283cc5ffc96dc04826aeab1131e95d3a2e64.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the closed path along the rectangle, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown a