9 "q-124393" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259687 1071 "$5!\\,0!=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259687 1072 #uuid "6688982b-061e-548a-92f3-135ff8f3c6b0" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259687 1074 "The given expression is a product of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5!=5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=120.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5!\\,0! & =120\\cdot 1=120\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259142 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1069 "q-124405" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1071 "$5!-0!=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1072 #uuid "d53009d8-333f-5b2e-8589-e17e64e488ba" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259688 1074 "The given expression is a subtraction of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5!=5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=120.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5!-0! & =120-1=119\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259304 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1069 "q-124409" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1071 "$0!+5!=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1072 #uuid "dcd48373-682a-59a9-b5fa-04669fe0cfbc" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259689 1074 "The given expression is a sum of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $5!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n5!=5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=120.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0!+5! & =1+120=121\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259387 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1069 "q-124410" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1071 "$3!-0!=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1072 #uuid "bf7bd909-cf27-508b-8907-199d014908fd" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259690 1074 "The given expression is a subtraction of two factorials.\n\n- Computing $3!$ using the definition, we get\n\n$$\n3!=3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=6.\n$$\n\n- By definition, the factorial of zero equals one:\n\n$$\n0!=1\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the given expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3!-0! & =6-1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259155 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1069 "q-124466" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1071 "![](images/47/4784e18b0e3631272efa1c002e686e9349ea04a900d8bc1e6b0416c09e994ccb.png)\n\nGiven that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3},$ calculate the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1072 #uuid "7a611cbb-f2d0-5dff-a92a-655b9e737441" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259691 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3},$ where $\\theta =m∠B.$ Notice we're also given the length of the side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to $∠B.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to find the length of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{AC}{AB} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\frac{AC}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 18 & =AC \\\\ AC & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 30\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =270.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259329 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1069 "q-124470" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1071 "![](images/5c/5c7116b4ee933aacb284e0d9b16576b910166bf1e01f58a46931fc0636a933c5.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1072 #uuid "84c7413b-4724-5493-849a-e288b034cec7" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259692 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{5x+2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{5x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(5x+2) & =3\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\,x+2\\sqrt{3} & =6\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2=4\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =5x+2=5(2)+2=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot BC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259397 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1069 "q-124471" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1071 "![](images/f0/f0d4e8ddf3a1b6cacab75f3207575695de5790f8223e30bc948e93daf37c939f.png)\n\nIn square units rounded to one decimal place, find the area of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1072 #uuid "21b99ac7-c59c-521c-b41f-4de6f2fb69c7" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259693 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x-1}{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})(x+2) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-x & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-1) & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})) & =1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})}{1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})} \\\\ x & =5.098\\,0…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =x-1=5.098\\,0-1=4.098\\,0…, \\\\ AB & =x+2=5.098\\,0+2=7.098\\,0….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}(4.098\\,0…)(7.098\\,0…) \\\\  & =14.543\\,8… \\\\  & ≈14.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the one decimal place." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259474 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1069 "q-124475" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1071 "![](images/0a/0ad5279a7ec9ac20a2c4e1449f93ae67854caf021aba8435165b80069f23afbd.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1072 #uuid "9cbd5c1c-b243-5354-99fa-1a41e7c9ebe7" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259694 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠B={60}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠B,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{AC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+2) & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+2\\sqrt{3} & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2=4\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+2=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259473 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1069 "q-124558" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1071 "![](images/43/43eb119ef43e136682d4451e4f85f007c63568f7a34880c46f87ab45122f5dd0.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=1-u,\\,y=2-v.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1072 #uuid "8d4da39e-0907-5e10-8ce6-cd308b5a0917" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259695 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-u \\\\ 2-v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-u \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/a8/a8a13c579c3ca8cc32f997c48e237c38d449aa07040de658589fa5cc68a02c55.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(2,2),(-1,2),(-1,1),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/14/146f9f36deeac0f3a7108a173485eb6d0d03138d18998a96dc9c3566bee21ca2.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=1>0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259046 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1069 "q-124561" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1071 "![](images/79/79b09bcf02998adf6d7bf19e7d59cabdc563ff26266966440bbb49be53029ac3.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-\\frac{u+1}{2}\\le v\\le u+1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1072 #uuid "6fdc3f93-8486-56f6-8744-50fa49ca621b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259696 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/53/539c74bf4d397916fd7ac11089fa9500bd71cdf2278891136785d6e792431b05.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,-1),(1,-1),$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/ad/adfe476bd2ca719996d612855ca5368bdea09370b7befcffb08a062db8587cbb.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259330 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1069 "q-124570" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1071 "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $9$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1072 #uuid "9f7eeecc-f096-52fa-83d2-c52d8199d233" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259697 1074 "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=9$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259145 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1069 "q-124571" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1071 "![](images/9b/9bfb4e21205db9550d88222e04449b9858137550b957ba652b3e6998dce8025c.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{1-v}{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1072 #uuid "0f649fab-02d4-543e-a9d4-f28134403418" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259698 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation is already in matrix form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/19/194428bede19351ba77866a9ca49f76b38c4327fd0a5e2c219afff9c14b12c28.png)\n\n             \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our triangle:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-2,0),(-1,-1),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/02/02ab748669496bf50a8809cf822a969d731a18283d52272175fd5b615ef5b765.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=2>0,$ this transformation preserves orientation." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259299 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1069 "q-124608" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1072 #uuid "e7d8485d-2f78-5d59-9916-1d1cd2c548de" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259699 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{2}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259399 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1069 "q-124611" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1072 #uuid "66205715-ba82-541e-8ac0-31ae0009bc90" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259700 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259343 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1069 "q-124613" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1071 "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1072 #uuid "19802aad-5d1c-554b-bf69-ef21c3999563" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259701 1074 "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259166 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1069 "q-12462" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1072 #uuid "1a4ea9ed-1853-52ed-ab6f-46794d76e304" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259702 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot (-{e}^{-x})-\\int (-{e}^{-x})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3x{e}^{-x}+3\\int {e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3x{e}^{-x}-3{e}^{-x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259323 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1069 "q-124625" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1071 "![](images/e5/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1072 #uuid "371bdeca-3a35-5956-9fa6-e8d03505d26b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259703 1074 "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259368 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1069 "q-124650" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1071 "![](images/d9/d947e13c2b18ce786f03636d174604dd60e715ed0ce1929ff4ac98cf91084a50.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u+2v-2,\\,y=2u+v-1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1072 #uuid "ef63f646-24fc-5651-a4a2-9022204b07a9" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259704 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v-2 \\\\ 2u+v-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u+2v \\\\ 2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](images/d0/d0103ef8cdf76a3f80bd9be6314c2a693cbf9e51fe66f95228ccce1703f6775f.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-2,-1),(1,1),(3,2),$ and $(0,0).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/0b/0b22301fac2a0d97c0f0d99b3c4d162e8965f5570522754a3e21fa124194f1c0.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=-1<0,$ this transformation is not orientation-preserving." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259349 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1069 "q-124652" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(-1,2)$ to the point $B(1,0),$ then ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1072 #uuid "c9d024fb-1731-5e0e-8fd1-3a79afb84b21" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259705 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j$ and $b=i.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(i-(-i+2\\,j)) \\\\  & =-i+2\\,j+t(2\\,i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t-1$ and $y(t)=2-2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,dx+dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)(2)+(-2)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-1)(2-2t)-1\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}-4{t}^{2}+6t-3\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[-\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+3{t}^{2}-3t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}+3-3]-2[0] \\\\  & =2[-\\frac{4}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259045 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1069 "q-124656" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1072 #uuid "1324f71e-2d99-5801-9cdc-c3861396a17d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259706 1074 "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-16{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t(1-4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259215 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1069 "q-124657" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,2).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1072 #uuid "b45898cb-232a-5e7b-9fc7-75fcf27a9882" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259707 1074 "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{2}{y}\\,dx+y{e}^{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{2}{y}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+y{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{2}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-1\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259339 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1069 "q-124658" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1071 "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(4,2),$ then ${\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1072 #uuid "d143a12d-610a-5ab9-bde5-0709d25859cb" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259708 1074 "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0\\,i+0\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+2\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0\\,i+0\\,j+t(4\\,i+2\\,j-(0\\,i+0\\,j)) \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=4t$ and $y(t)=2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=4\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx-{e}^{x+y}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{e}^{x+y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x+y}(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{4t+2t}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6t}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6(1)}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{6(0)}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{6}-1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259438 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1069 "q-124661" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-2,0).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1072 #uuid "3189421a-c22a-5fa2-87db-a7498ce87212" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259709 1074 "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+