=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-y)\\,dx+(x+y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}((x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}t({t}^{2}-2t)+({t}^{2}+2t)\\,dt \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2[(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}+{2}^{2})-(\\frac{{1}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+{1}^{2})] \\\\  & =2[4-\\frac{8}{3}+4-\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3}-1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{53}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{53}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259146 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1069 "q-124665" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-3).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1072 #uuid "0e2357f5-0311-5b8a-87b7-eaae763c04c4" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259712 1074 "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}dx+\\frac{2}{x}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{2}{x}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+6\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6t]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+9\\pi )-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +6\\pi ) \\\\  & =(0+9\\pi )-(-1+6\\pi ) \\\\  & =3\\pi +1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259043 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1069 "q-124668" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1071 "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{3}$ traversed from the point $P(1,1)$ to point $Q(2,8),$ then ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy=$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1072 #uuid "170a6ced-a411-5130-91d2-063d0a1b4a48" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259713 1074 "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{3}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{3}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+3{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y}\\,dx+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}+3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}}+3t]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{2({2}^{2})}+3(2)]-[-\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}+3(1)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+6+\\frac{1}{2}-3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259435 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1069 "q-124669" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=(x+z)\\,i+(z-y)\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1072 #uuid "f0df9e72-05b2-5d70-9436-a25cbf3016bb" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259714 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=3{t}^{2},\\,z=2t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+4{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)j \\\\  & =2ti+6t\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+4{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (2ti+6t\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+12{t}^{2}-18{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}-16{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(24{t}^{2}-16{t}^{3})\\,dt \\\\  & ={8{t}^{3}-4{t}^{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259280 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1069 "q-124670" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy,$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)=2t\\,i+(2-{t}^{2})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,1].$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1072 #uuid "774a7e23-2066-572e-b119-9d6d06d53bda" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259715 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2t,\\,y(t)=2-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}]dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t+2-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+2t\\cdot (-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-6{t}^{2}+4t+4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[-2{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2+2+4)-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259374 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1069 "q-124677" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+z\\,j+y\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{6}].$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1072 #uuid "35a18d1f-abe9-5e26-930e-866e387b2c48" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259716 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+z\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,j-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)}{4}|}_{0}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259377 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1069 "q-124679" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(y+1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1072 #uuid "34976ea5-f60a-5f5a-a35d-aa51a7c27ec6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259717 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+(y+1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259132 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1069 "q-124688" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that  $F(x,y)=x\\,i+(y-1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1072 #uuid "8ff38139-bd4b-55b1-a4ac-c9a0d7dde49f" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259718 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+(y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & 