rue] [17592186259826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259386 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259826 1069 "q-125507" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259826 1071 "![](images/86/866a1812bd31e0443becf35a7cc2b8df1bc84a49091a94250b60fe3533e4b172.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259826 1072 #uuid "91c88aa7-9c4e-5dd7-9c30-608f94c03554" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259826 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4232577b0687d781f702a196ae2c0060d3264c1f44a45a404a0f9da03bc0dd1.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/f7/f7cfcef37ed6a52d5bd374fae2d7a00c4d8012e9ea8dbef43165c10bcc9471be.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259055 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1069 "q-12552" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1071 "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events. Given that $P(A)=0.5$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.7,$ find $P(B).$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1072 #uuid "1aeb6fdb-442c-5d80-8d96-6a5dfca935bd" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259827 1074 "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events, then $P(A\\cap B)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, by the addition law, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B) \\\\ P(B) & =P(A\\cup B)-P(A) \\\\  & =0.7-0.5 \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259106 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1069 "q-125527" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1071 "What are the dimensions of the matrix $S$, where $S=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -5 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1072 #uuid "8c21a537-8833-5fb1-9faa-0f3b2f1af56b" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259828 1074 "The matrix $S$ has $6$ rows and $6$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $6\\times 6.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259405 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1069 "q-125528" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1071 "What are the dimensions of the matrix $T$, where $T=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5} & -23 \\\\ 4 & -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1072 #uuid "7783a7fa-d703-5aad-927d-29508fc5f399" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259829 1074 "The matrix $T$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259125 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1069 "q-125529" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1071 "What are the dimensions of the matrix $Y$, where $Y=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{7} & 10 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1072 #uuid "035fc963-2ee7-5299-9898-0c721b8439ff" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259830 1074 "The matrix $Y$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259305 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1069 "q-12554" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1071 "A card is drawn from a standard $52$-card deck. The drawn card is the Ace of Spades and it is set aside. A second card is drawn from the deck. What is the probability that the drawn card is an ace or a spade?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Spades is one of the suits.*" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1072 #uuid "4d422248-baa9-5e0e-a9ed-604bdfcad48a" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259831 1074 "Let us call $A$ the event \"The second card drawn is an ace,\" and $S$ the event \"The second card drawn is a spade.\" The required probability is given by  $P(A\\cup S).$ \n\nGiven that the Ace of Spades is no longer in the deck, $A$ and $S$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(A\\cap S)=0.$\n\nSince the Ace of Spades was set aside, there are $51$ cards remaining,  of which $3$ are aces and $12$ are spades. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{3}{51}, \\\\ P(S) & =\\frac{12}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup S) & =P(A)+P(S)-P(A\\cap S) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{51}+\\frac{12}{51}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+12}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{51} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259158 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1069 "q-125543" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1071 "What are the dimensions of the matrix $Y$, where $Y=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1072 #uuid "85229588-ff49-5ee6-8e12-af80849ca1af" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259832 1074 "The matrix $Y$ has $1$ row  and $1$ column.  So,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 1.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1069 "q-125552" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1071 "Which of the following is a $2\\times 1$ matrix?\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1072 #uuid "0cf927e3-7417-58b7-b060-c44ad3d85430" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259833 1074 "- The matrix $B$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $2$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 1.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $D$ has $1$ row and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259164 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1069 "q-12556" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1072 #uuid "d57f8c60-94ea-5515-8561-35af7ac97700" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259834 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int x{e}^{-3x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{3})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x{e}^{-3x}}{3}-\\frac{{e}^{-3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259179 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1069 "q-125585" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1071 "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1072 #uuid "205601cc-6f43-54e3-8d21-d33b726485ab" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259835 1074 "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259242 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1069 "q-12559" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1071 "Calculate $\\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1072 #uuid "a269e9e4-ba48-5393-8b91-051e5b1bc073" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259836 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x+5\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-5x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (3x+5)\\cdot {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(3x+5)\\cdot (-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5})-\\int (-\\frac{{e}^{-5x}}{5})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3x+5){e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}+\\frac{3}{5}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3x+5){e}^{-5x}}{5}-\\frac{3{e}^{-5x}}{25}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259350 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1069 "q-12560" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1072 #uuid "b258b659-45f7-5842-b37a-ee530a2eb1b6" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259837 1074 "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x+7\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (x+7)\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =(x+7)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+7){e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7){e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259267 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1069 "q-125630" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1071 "Calculate $P+Q$, where\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1072 #uuid "1d66a419-6411-5c1d-98a2-c12810fcee94" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259838 1074 "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1 & (-2)+0 \\\\ 0+0 & (-3)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259294 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1069 "q-125631" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1071 "Calculate $X+Y$, where\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1072 #uuid "67f826d9-b18f-53e7-9f32-2aa51105b142" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259839 1074 "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+0 & 0+(-1) \\\\ 0+(-2) & 1+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259217 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1069 "q-125637" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1071 "![](images/e6/e60f7c0adc3e2ec8daa3203564c31bf660e6eca7e6394955cc73133ac3673ed5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1072 #uuid "547c79d0-6e8c-5db1-9245-a09abf6b78b5" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259840 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/a8/a8010db8481768cb61d0734dcfc97eed646441ab265c4928d06d274ef0e1c1dd.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/be/be263d706bfc3ef205be37aaed85d4984d3739d53cdaadb8cba88e3d5196ce93.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259453 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1069 "q-125643" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1071 "![](images/f4/f4042d4eb43310480f3c75673befbf58067a89704d20b9a63f0f6c7272179f3e.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1072 #uuid "982dcf8b-4c96-5db9-9ec4-1bcce43696b3" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259841 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/fa/fabbbb78d8fd4d327fe82d1d93d8e0fc15dd33e909266e0ef88de931be28b951.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement I is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/71/712564e3b0ad0e23d2d9097eb02c0857130bdaa6619a005738205130ab66ac3a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259300 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1069 "q-125647" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1072 #uuid "7b7ddac2-e3bd-590b-a396-56e8108da7f7" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259842 1074 "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct 