tive vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement III is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259429 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1069 "q-125651" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{xy}}{{x}^{2}+1}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1072 #uuid "09907334-a616-5854-99eb-0e8232bc08ee" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259845 1074 "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement III is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259101 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1069 "q-125652" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={5}^{-x-y}+\\sqrt{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1072 #uuid "cfd2b36c-080f-5929-a5a6-dd070c8ce235" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259846 1074 "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259425 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1069 "q-12566" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1071 "Calculate $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1072 #uuid "6d30efc2-05d5-57a7-a888-f5dbfc3f16de" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259847 1074 "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {v}^{′}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡(x))(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡(x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259331 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1069 "q-125664" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+3x)\\,i+(2xy+3)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1072 #uuid "2353653c-eb1d-5d31-b3c0-eed1015199de" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259848 1074 "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259094 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1069 "q-125668" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1071 "![](images/6e/6ed50407066da9fc213202ccff8c8be06c89d2dce0f5dfe07877cab77c98b3ec.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1072 #uuid "bb28647b-13b7-5f23-9064-5edced19689d" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259849 1074 "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/04/045f6867a50dff0c162376015cc4651de796d0a637643b4b5ba402ef55dde4fa.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/69/698f68b9539e6ef5c4b07dbe6cf3859449e2687db19406f6c2b0072fa5eb697e.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259362 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1069 "q-125688" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1071 "![](images/b9/b9dd2fb4478ac443020e9cbebd64a2c9e4cdf5c7b6d5ce5cede854e0d7a3d804.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1072 #uuid "dc38a3d9-696c-596f-b353-c55f7e71f2d3" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259850 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/51/51fafa71ff31592d9692348087cf0a20f6bbaa3b05e8519b4109d44207899a39.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/22/2287c33ff81973bcaef70917452c97282e0d0dd9ef06141d5beee8b53dec5836.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259141 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1069 "q-125706" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1071 "![](images/a4/a423ab56220e438fb849d2025ab53b99bb74ef598e16110c911d691680130527.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1072 #uuid "e9d575c8-adec-5386-bb2f-10bc638decac" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259851 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works  against the particle for $x<0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x>0.$ \n\n![](images/cd/cd52e91c72d481980c9a48575da7ca3eaebd2ed27f96b1054cf5c1c5da1aa3d9.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a1/a1e8fa5262fc787b691b3753fc57ab39aceee7c57dbbe166c6da6f6effaf42dc.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259480 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1069 "q-125722" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1071 "Calculate $P+Q,$ where\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1072 #uuid "5df39415-9d38-5a3a-9412-09ec30f0c75f" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259852 1074 "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P+Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2 & (-1)+(-3) \\\\ (-4)+1 & 0+1 \\\\ (-1)+2 & 2+(-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186259084 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1069 "q-125723" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative.\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n- ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1072 #uuid "769cb3f6-44e5-5633-bebf-2f89e5fbe161" 13194139533398 17592186045585 true] [17592186259853 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{{\\